    </div>

    <h2>What makes Level 51 tricky<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Three of the fourteen moves start with a family member instead of a prop. Step 1 drags the grandfather onto the deflated tire, step 9 drags the mother into the wooden house, step 13 drags the grandfather onto the motorcycle, and the garage is opened one card after she goes in. The same man is handled like an object at both ends, parked on a tire first and loaded onto a bike last. A run that treats the family as scenery looks for a tool to drag onto the flat tire and finds nothing, because the pump in this yard is aimed at a balloon on a branch, not at the tire sitting next to it.</p>
    <p>The two combine moves are both two-stage, and neither input is a finished tool. The balloon only becomes carryable once the pump has been dragged onto it, which is why step 3 hands over the inflated balloon rather than the balloon. The branch is scenery until step 7, when it is dragged onto the shovel head on the ground and turns into the shovel the father needs one card later. In both cases a new name appears in the next card, and that name is your next object. The window in step 5 and the garage in step 10 are the only clicks in fourteen moves; everything else is a drag.</p>
    <p>The ending is a relay rather than a single gesture. Gasoline and the tire both go onto the motorcycle, at steps 11 and 12, the grandfather climbs on at 13, and the level still is not finished, because the grandmother is standing off to the side waiting for a mushroom at step 14. Thirteen moves build a getaway and the fourteenth is a snack. If the scene looks complete and the polaroid screen has not appeared, check what the grandmother is holding. The check line shows the same spread: garage at 1:05, mushroom at 1:10, completion banner at 1:20.</p>

    <h2>What stalls a Level 51 run<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Six things hold up a run here, and five of them look like scenery:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The pump. It goes to the balloon on the branch; the flat tire under the grandfather is not its target.</li>
      <li>The branch. Dragged onto the shovel head on the ground, it becomes the shovel the father takes at step 8.</li>
      <li>The window and the garage. Both are clicks on the house, and step 10 only comes after the mother has been dragged inside at step 9.</li>
      <li>The motorcycle. Gasoline at 11, tire at 12, grandfather at 13: three separate drags onto one vehicle.</li>
      <li>The grandmother. She is not part of the getaway and only moves at step 14.</li>
      <li>The mushroom. It is the smallest object in the yard and the last one the cards ask for.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Once the branch has become a shovel there is nothing left to combine, only a motorcycle to load and one mushroom to hand over.</p>

    <h2>FAQ<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
   
    <details><summary>What is the answer to Brain Puzzle 3 Level 51?</summary><p>In order: 1) Drag the grandfather to the deflated tire; 2) Drag the pump to the balloon on the branch; 3) Drag the inflated balloon to the younger brother; 4) Drag the paint to the older sister; 5) Click the window of the wooden house; 6) Drag the ninja outfit to the older brother; 7) Drag the branch to the shovel head on the ground; 8) Drag the shovel to the father; 9) Drag the mother to the wooden house; 10) Click the garage of the wooden house; 11) Drag the gasoline to the motorcycle; 12) Drag the tire to the motorcycle; 13) Drag the grandfather onto the motorcycle; 14) Drag the mushroom to the grandmother</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why does nothing happen when I tap in Level 51?</summary><p>In Level 51 the taps that do nothing are the window and the garage, which are the only two clicks; every other card, including the ones that start on a family member, is a drag.</p></details>
